Link: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4446134

## My definition of bioinformatics
1. Bioinformatics is the use of computer tools to store, analyse and
   interpret biological datasets.
2. Researchers need computers because biological datasets are too
   large to analyse by eye.
3. Computers also produce results much faster, which matters in
   outbreaks and for patients.
4. Bioinformatics can predict which drugs a pathogen is resistant to,
   which helps in choosing treatment.
5. Examples are COVID-19, tuberculosis and malaria.

## Three infectious disease examples
1. COVID-19: researchers compared thousands of virus genomes to track
   how the virus was changing.
2. Tuberculosis: a tool read whole-genome sequence data from an
   isolate and predicted which anti-TB drugs it would resist.
3. Malaria: [one sentence, in your own words]

## Three ways computing helped in the paper
1. It made it possible to analyse a whole genome, because a TB genome
   has about 4.4 million letters, which is too large to compare by eye.
2. The mutations found were compared to a library of 1,325 known
   resistance markers, which is too large to check by hand.
3. The tool gave a prediction of which drugs an isolate is resistant
   to, so that a patient can start effective treatment sooner.

## New words I learned
- In silico: done by computer
- Whole-genome sequencing (WGS): reading all the DNA letters of an organism
- Reference genome: a standard sequence used for comparison (for TB, H37Rv)
- FASTQ: the file format that holds raw sequencing reads
- Isolate: a microbe grown from one patient's sample

